From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 20 Sep 2026 10:50:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] AMLLatency: put the displayed timestamp in the same epoch as
 the clock

pts_video is the kernel's 32-bit 90kHz counter. It restarts every 2^32 ticks,
about every thirteen hours, while the playback clock keeps running, so once a
long-running session has crossed a wrap the two are a whole period apart and
the alignment this reports is that period wrong.

The frame being handed over and the frame on screen are a few frames apart, so
lift the displayed timestamp into the period nearest the clock reading it is
being subtracted from. Within one epoch this rounds to zero and changes
nothing.

This figure is published for diagnostics and does not steer playback, so the
symptom is a reading nobody can believe rather than a picture anybody can see.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

--- a/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DVDCodecs/Video/AMLLatency.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/cores/VideoPlayer/DVDCodecs/Video/AMLLatency.cpp
@@ -595,7 +595,14 @@
     }
     age += carried;
 
-    const double shownUs = static_cast<double>(m_pollTicks) * (DVD_TIME_BASE / 90000.0);
+    // pts_video is the kernel's 32-bit 90kHz counter, so it restarts every
+    // 2^32 ticks - about 13 hours - while the playback clock runs on. Lift it
+    // into the period the frame being handed over sits in; the two are a few
+    // frames apart, so the nearest period is the right one.
+    double shownUs = static_cast<double>(m_pollTicks) * (DVD_TIME_BASE / 90000.0);
+    constexpr double ptsWrapUs = 4294967296.0 * (DVD_TIME_BASE / 90000.0);
+    const double shownRef = clockNow - age;
+    shownUs += std::round((shownRef - shownUs) / ptsWrapUs) * ptsWrapUs;
     const double alignment = clockNow - age - shownUs +
                              static_cast<double>(RDMA_COMMIT_VBLANKS) * m_period;
     CAMLLatencyStore::GetInstance().SetAlignment(static_cast<float>(alignment / 1000.0));
